From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nsiproxy: the Tcpip6 interface keys and the DHCPv6 lease, as Windows' DHCPv6 client keeps them (9211)

9252 made Tcpip6\Parameters\Interfaces; nothing went in it (ledger i0511). Now every adapter gets its Tcpip6\Parameters\Interfaces\{GUID} key, as on Windows, and with a DHCPv6 lease (NetworkManager's [dhcp6]: dhcp6_name_servers, dhcp6_domain_search, iaid, dhcp6_client_id) its Dhcpv6Iaid (REG_DWORD), Dhcpv6DNSServers (REG_BINARY, 16-byte addresses) and Dhcpv6DomainSearchList; the client's DUID is the machine's, Tcpip6\Parameters\Dhcpv6DUID (REG_BINARY), kept once known. Only changed values are written; a gone adapter's key goes with its Tcpip one. dnsapi reads Dhcpv6DNSServers as the adapter's IPv6 name servers (9210).

Gate: test-tcpipreg. Mutant SG_MUTANT_TCPIP6_NO_DHCP6.
---
--- a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/nsiproxy_private.h
+++ b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/nsiproxy_private.h
@@ -113,6 +113,15 @@
     UINT bus;               /* the device behind the interface: 0 none (virtual), 1 PCI, 2 USB */
     UINT hw_vendor, hw_device, hw_subvendor, hw_subdevice, hw_revision;
     char hw_location[64];   /* the bus address: PCI 0000:04:00.0, USB 1-2 */
+    /* the DHCPv6 lease (NetworkManager's [dhcp6]), for Tcpip6 (9211, i0511) */
+    BOOL dhcp6;
+    UINT iaid;              /* the identity association's ID; 0 unknown */
+    BOOL have_iaid;
+    BYTE duid[130];         /* the client's DUID (option 1) */
+    UINT duid_len;
+    BYTE dns6[4][16];       /* the lease's name servers (option 23) */
+    UINT dns6_count;
+    char search6[256];      /* the lease's domain search list (option 24) */
 };
 
